Overlay patch 0031: stereoscopic eye rendering for visionOS 3D (D-030 M3/M4).
The shell's CompositorServices loop (SohImmersive.m) composites two per-eye
Metal textures onto a world-locked panel. This patch makes Fast3D produce
them: in 3D mode (gSoh3DMode, shell-owned), Fast3dWindow runs the interpreter
TWICE per host frame — same game time, same command list — with an eye state
that (a) swaps mGameFb to a per-eye framebuffer (all existing offscreen
plumbing then targets it transparently), and (b) applies an off-axis stereo
projection to PERSPECTIVE matrices only (vkQuake patches/0010 math, row-vector
transposed): camera shift folded into P as P'[3][j] -= e*P'[0][j], and the
convergence skew P'[2][0] -= P'[0][0]*e/C so zero parallax sits at C — ortho
(HUD/2D) stays untouched and therefore exactly on the panel plane. The screen
framebuffer (fb 0) is NEVER touched in 3D: no drawable acquire (a hidden
window's acquire stalls forever), no present — a new Metal EndFrameOffscreen
commits the drawn framebuffers, runs 0019's pending-mip pass, and does
EndFrame's state cleanup. Both eyes render EVERY host frame (alternating
halves each eye's rate and reads as judder). Engine dims are overridden to
the eye render size during a pass and restored on exit. CVars:
rev2 (device feedback): convergence is ADAPTIVE — anchored on the smoothed
camera-to-focus distance exported by 0032, biased by gSohIos.StereoConvBias
(default 1.0); the eye offset is gSohIos.StereoDepth (default 0.025) TIMES
the convergence, so background disparity is scene-constant. Fixed sep/conv
made depth swing wildly with OoT's camera distance (uphill-intense,
field-flat, near-ground diplopia — all one root cause).
rev12 (VR R0, VR-spec D2): the SAME injection seam also carries VR. When
gSohVRMode is set the shell publishes a complete per-eye A.V.P matrix each
compositor frame (row-vector, Fast3D order) and Soh3DStereoP SUBSTITUTES it
for the game's combined view*projection rather than skewing it — ortho/HUD
still keeps the game's own matrix, so the perspective-only gate is shared.
This lands as a REGENERATION of 0031 rather than a stacked patch: the seam is
inside text 0031 itself inserts, and a patch stacked onto patch-inserted text
breaks 0031's reverse probe in apply-overlay.sh (SpaghettiKart VR-R0 finding).
rev13 (VR R1, VR-spec D2/D3/D8): three additions, all VR-gated.
(a) POSE RENDEZVOUS. The shell now publishes a LIVE pose every compositor
frame, so the matrices can no longer be read straight out of a shared array
while the interpreter walks it. gSohVREyeVP becomes DOUBLE-BUFFERED
([slot][eye][16]) and the host-frame branch latches gSohVRPoseSeq -- and
therefore one slot -- ONCE for the whole two-eye pair, so both eyes are
guaranteed to come from the same head pose. On completion it publishes
gSohVREyeSeqDone, which the compositor loop waits on with a bounded timeout
before presenting (timeout -> it re-presents the previous pair against the
anchor that pair was rendered with).
(b) FLAT-SCREEN CONTEXTS. gSohVRFlatLatch (overlay 0039, latched at the tick
boundary before the DL is built) is latched again for the pair; while set,
Soh3DStereoP leaves the game's own matrix alone, so a flat frame renders MONO
and lands on the world-locked panel instead of being smeared across the world.
(c) DEPTH HANDOFF. The eye framebuffer's DEPTH texture is published beside its
colour texture on GPU completion, so the compositor blit can convert Fast3D's
forward-Z depth into the drawable's reverse-Z/infinite-far target (R0 finding
4: the conventions genuinely differ, so this is a conversion, never a copy).
rev14 (VR R2a, device round, trap D35): THE EYE PAIR IS THE UNIT, NOT THE EYE.
rev11 publishes each eye texture from its OWN GPU completion handler, so the
compositor -- which reads gSoh3DEyeTexture[0] and [1] independently -- can hold
eye 0 from host frame N and eye 1 from host frame N-1, submit ONE device anchor
for the two, and hand the headset two eyes rendered for two different head
poses. That is diplopia no projection math can fix, and it is the leading
candidate for the user's "everything is doubled" on the first device build. It is
invisible in the simulator, whose serialized queues publish both eyes together.
Fix: every published eye carries the PAIR TAG (gSohVRPairTag, the pose seq the
host-frame branch latched for that pair), and a new
Soh3DEyePairSnapshot(tex, depth, tag) hands the shell both eyes' textures,
depths and tags in ONE critical section under the existing publish mutex. The
shell adopts a pair only when the two tags match and otherwise re-presents the
last coherent pair against that pair's own anchor.
rev15 (VR R2b, spec D7 + the rainbow finding): TWO additions.
(a) THE HUD PLANE. OoT's interface -- the in-play HUD, the scene title card,
dialogue boxes, the title/intro overlay -- is TEXRECT output: screen-space
vertices built by GfxDpTextureRectangle that never touch MP_matrix, so the
projection injector cannot reach them and the ortho gate leaves them on the
game's own matrix. Both eyes therefore get IDENTICAL PIXEL POSITIONS, and under
the device's asymmetric per-eye frusta identical pixels are not the same
direction -- the HUD doubles and drifts to the edges (the user, 1.0.1.2). Overlay
0043 unchains the overlay display list in VR gameplay; a THIRD interpreter walk
(sSoh3DEye == 3) renders it once per host frame into its own 4:3 framebuffer,
cleared TRANSPARENT (Soh3DSetFbClearAlphaZero), published in slot 2 through the
same completion machinery as the eyes and carrying the same pair tag. The shell
composites it as a flat plane at a real distance with each eye's own
projection, which is what gives it true disparity.
(b) THE VERTEX BUFFER POOL. kMaxVertexBufferPoolSize is 3, sized for ONE
offscreen pass per host frame; VR does two (three with the HUD) and nothing
fences the CPU's memcpy into a pool slot against the GPU still reading it. See
the comment in gfx_metal.h -- this is the best explanation on the table for the
rainbow polygons the user sees only while the Mac Virtual Display is in view.
iOS gets 8 slots; desktop keeps 3.
All new code is __IOS__-guarded; the shell globals live in SohIosShell.m so
iPhone links resolve (mode simply stays 0 there); macOS compiles none of it.
rev3 (VR R1): the macOS build could not LINK. These extern declarations are
unguarded on purpose -- the code they gate is behaviour-gated by gSoh3DMode
rather than compiled out -- but the strong definitions live in the iOS shell
(app/ios/SohIosShell.m), which no desktop build links. So every unguarded
reference now carries a WEAK zeroed default beside it, compiled only when
__IOS__ is absent. The shell's strong definitions win wherever it IS linked
(weak loses to strong), and on desktop the flag is a constant 0, so the gated
paths are unreachable exactly as intended. Found when the Q-VR5 fmt/ZAPD
blocker was cleared and soh-macos reached the link step for the first time.
rev19 (VR R11 verdict 1): A SKYBOX HAS NO POSITION. the user, on 1.0.1.14: "Sky
still pulsates like crazy when I move." R7 seated the sky matrix on the game
camera and R10 on the head; both are POSITIONS, and both failed for one reason
neither round wrote down. A skybox vertex lands in eye space at
S * R^T * (R_sky*p_model + M_t - seat), and the sphere radius |p_model| = 126
units is multiplied by the SAME S -- so the 100x "effective infinity" scale
divides the residual and the radius alike and changes no angle at all. The
error is |M_t - seat| / 126 radians whatever the scale, M_t is the game camera
as Fast3D INTERPOLATED it for this host frame, and no seat we can compute is
sampled on the same clock: ten units of disagreement is 4.5 degrees of swim.
The fix removes translation from the path entirely -- the shell publishes a sky
pose whose translation is zero, and this rev zeroes the MODEL matrix's
translation row for draws inside 0033's skybox sentinel (Soh3DSkyModel /
Soh3DSkyPath, at both MP composition sites). What is left is
S * R^T * R_sky * p_model: the sphere centred on the eye by construction, both
eyes sharing one rotation so the disparity stays exactly zero. The dropped
translation is published as gSohVRSkyMDrop for `vr sky`.
rev20 (VR R18 part B, D-071): THE ASPECT CORRECTION BELONGS TO THE DISPLAY LIST,
NOT TO THE PASS. the user, on 1.0.1.22: the Lens of Truth "should show the same red
interface with a circle like vanilla" -- and in VR its circle is an oval with
clipped sides. AdjXForAspectRatio multiplies every screen-space texrect edge AND
every vertex's clip x by (4/3)/aspect, reading mCurDimensions, i.e. the dims of
the pass being RENDERED. rev15 leaves those dims at the HUD plane's 4:3
1600x1200 between host frames on purpose, so the GAME thread builds its display
list while GetAspectRatio() reports 4:3: OTRGetRectDimensionFromLeftEdge(0) is
0 and OTRGetRectDimensionFromRightEdge(SCREEN_WIDTH) is SCREEN_WIDTH, a rect that
already spans the whole field, and then the eye pass stretches it by
(4/3)/eye_aspect anyway. Hence the oval.
The factor is now taken against the aspect the DL was BUILT with -- sampled once
per host frame by Soh3DNoteBuildAspect() before the first eye pass moves dims --
rather than by restoring eye dims after the HUD pass, which would move the
pillarboxing onto the HUD instead.
TWO CONSEQUENCES, SAID OUT LOUD. (a) Every world-list screen-space rect
un-stretches with the lens: Environment_FillScreen's flashes and fades,
Oceff_Storm, z_room's fills, the game-over/void-out washes. (b) So does the
WORLD: in VR Soh3DStereoP substitutes the shell's exact per-eye frustum for the
game's whole view*projection, so a (4/3)/eye_aspect scale on clip x was pure
error there -- roughly 7 per cent of horizontal stretch at the device's 1.246
eye aspect. The oval circle is the proof it was live, since the mask and the
world go through the same multiply. It follows that the R17 aim trims (the bow's
yaw +25 especially) were measured through that stretch and may want
re-measuring; that is a headset question, not a host one.
The branch is a provable no-op everywhere else: eye 1 and eye 2 share dims with
the recorded value, the HUD pass's dims ARE the recorded value, and the 3D-panel
mode (no HUD plane) records the eye aspect it already used.

