+// SOH_IOS (overlay 0031 rev15, VR R2b): THE VERTEX BUFFER POOL IS SIZED FOR
+// ONE OFFSCREEN PASS PER HOST FRAME, AND VR RUNS THREE.
+// The pool index advances once per EndFrame/EndFrameOffscreen. Flat play
+// does one of those per frame, so a slot is reused three frames later and
+// the GPU has long finished with it. The VR loop does TWO (one per eye) --
+// three with rev15's HUD pass -- so with three slots a slot is rewritten
+// roughly every host frame, and nothing fences the CPU's memcpy into it
+// against the GPU still reading it. Whenever the GPU falls a frame behind
+// (which is exactly what happens when the compositor also has to render a
+// Mac Virtual Display window), live vertex data is overwritten mid-draw.
+// That is the signature the user photographed on 1.0.1.2: huge sheared
+// saturated triangles that appear only while the MVD is in view, and it is
+// the same artefact SpaghettiKart saw and 'cured' by shrinking its eye
+// targets (VR-R7-NOTES 5) -- shrinking cut GPU time, so the GPU stopped
+// falling behind; the pool was never the thing anyone looked at.
+// iOS/visionOS gets 8 slots (~2.7 host frames of slack at three passes,
+// 38 MB at this buffer size). Desktop keeps 3 byte-for-byte.
